// src/std/core/loop/loop_iter.zen
// one construct. The compiler picks the variant by signature — the
// ordinary overload rule, a closure's type being its full parameter
// list. No carve-out for closures, which is why Zen needs no `for`
// keyword and no second control-flow concept.
//
// LOOPS NEVER ALLOCATE: no declaration here takes an Alloc, and by
// law 1 that's the signature, not a promise. The two words that can
// allocate (loop_find.zen) take an Alloc and are different NAMES, not
// overloads — a generic `init: A` would swallow `alloc: Alloc`.
// Mechanically: a body is a non-escaping stack closure inlined at the
// call site (no closure record to box), index/acc thread BY VALUE (no
// heap cell across passes), and a fold over a stack array is a plain
// C for-loop. tests/bench/bench_loop.zen DECLARES that last one at
// allocs_op: 0, where a hit of 1 would mean an inliner regression
// boxing the body — but nothing measures it: scripts/bench.py reports
// allocs_op "unmeasured", and `bench` is not part of `make test`.
//
// EVERY DECLARATION HERE IS A SIGNATURE, not a definition — deliberate:
// a loop can't be written in terms of `.match`/`.loop`/recursion
// (circular, or riding an ungated tail-call optimisation). The
// compiler lowers each overload to a C loop directly. Everything
// ABOVE the primitive — find, filter, map — is ordinary Zen on top of
// it, proof h.break is a general feature.

LoopHandle = std.core.loop.loop_handle
Range      = std.core.range
Res        = std.core.result
Map        = std.collections

// while true. h.break is the only way out.
loop*<T> = (body: (h: LoopHandle) ()) Res<T>

// while true, with iteration counter — one more parameter; counter
// starts at 0 and threads by value.
loop*<T> = (body: (h: LoopHandle, index: usize) ()) Res<T>

// while cond, tested BEFORE each pass — a cond false to begin with
// runs the body zero times.
loop*<T> = (cond: () bool, body: (h: LoopHandle) ()) Res<T>

// ranged/collection iteration, with and without index. index is the
// pass count, value is the element — over Range(10, 13) these differ,
// and a body that confuses them prints the same one twice.
loop*<R: Range<T>, T> = (range: R, body: (
    h     : LoopHandle,
    index : usize,
    value : T
) ()) Res<T>
loop*<R: Range<T>, T> = (range: R, body: (h: LoopHandle, value: T) ()) Res<T>

// fold: init seeds acc, acc threads through iterations, the loop
// evaluates to the final acc — or to h.break(value), one form not two.
// init is the answer when there is nothing to fold.
loop*<R: Range<T>, T, A> = (range: R, init: A, body: (
    h     : LoopHandle,
    index : usize,
    value : T,
    acc   : A
) A) Res<A>

// key/value containers — A DIFFERENT NAME, not a seventh overload.
// DESIGN.md writes this as
//
//     loop*<K, V> = (map: Map<K, V>, body: (h, key: K, value: V) ())
//
// which can't resolve against the ranged-with-index form above: same
// arg count, same three-param body, first param generic in one,
// concrete in the other — exactly what DESIGN.md's overloading
// section rules out ("a generic parameter swallows a concrete one").
// Reported as a doc bug against DESIGN.md rather than resolved
// quietly here. Same trade `map` took: a Map hands out two things per
// pass, a Range one — never one word, so the name says which you get.
//
// The walk is Map.pairs (collections_map.zen), a MEMBER rather than a
// signature here: its body walks entries, which is module-private to
// collections_map on purpose — what a Map is made of is not part of
// what a Map is. This file only holds what the compiler is the body
// of; a walk with an ordinary Zen body belongs with the storage it
// reads.
